WEBVTT

00:00:00.978 --> 00:00:20.077
Good evening to you everyone. And I want to talk today about a special topic because when Jung is mentioned, very often Freud is also mentioned, not only because they lived at the same time, but because they also worked together and worked all also a very long time together.

00:00:21.119 --> 00:00:38.851
And just to put a little bit into a reference frame, there's a way to structure C.G. Jung's life into certain blocks and his life's work pretty much follows his own life. And there can be these blocks very neatly distinguished.

00:00:38.996 --> 00:01:00.938
And the first block in his life is the psychoanalytic block, uh, where Jung, uh, was a professor and also taught about psychoanalysis. He practiced, he worked with Freud very closely together. And this is in the first part till 38 or 39.

00:01:02.415 --> 00:01:24.319
There's the other part, the next one following, which is the part that most people know with the archetypes, the collective unconscious, anima, animus, where he explores and develops his ideas there. And the last part is the alchemical part where many people don't go, but where he also talks about Aions and synchronicity.

00:01:25.044 --> 00:01:45.418
So his ideas become more and more all-encompassing and yeah, complex to get into. And very beginning is the psychoanalysis part, which is personal because it's around patients. So what I will talk about.

00:01:47.728 --> 00:02:10.038
Will be very much colored by Jung's view on Freud. There's, in the Collected Works, for those who have it or want to have it, there's the fourth book. I have it here in German. It's called Freud and Psychoanalysis. And it's a neat little book because it really focuses on this topic of Freud and psychoanalysis.

00:02:11.038 --> 00:02:35.424
When Jung was thinking about how to structure his Collected Works and his life's work. His first impulse was to say, okay, when people should read me, they should read me in chronological sequence because then they, people can see, okay, what developed after what. And on a certain point of view, it makes sense.

00:02:35.488 --> 00:02:43.292
On the other, Jung talks about so many different things his whole life. That it's better to bundle these things. And this book is also a bundling.

00:02:43.825 --> 00:03:05.015
So here you have within one volume his very beginning views on psychoanalysis, where he is basically sending fan letters to Freud and he's helping him fight against critics.

00:03:05.526 --> 00:03:25.257
Then becoming a good friend of him, and then later changing his views. So it's a book that's spanning decades of Jung's personal development and his life and his views on psychoanalysis. But it's not only that which I read. I also read The Interpretation of Dreams by, uh, Freud.

00:03:26.157 --> 00:03:50.703
And I read an introduction into, uh, psychoanalysis also by Freud. Freud is a little bit easier to read because that's not so much. Freud did not write so many things, and he was more of a guy who would rewrite things, not for the topics, but rather he would expand and refine it a little bit.

00:03:51.139 --> 00:04:08.597
And you really notice it when you read The Interpretation of Dreams, which came out in 1899. He would publish revisions of that the next 10, 20 years. And it's a very well-structured book, so you really get the feeling that somebody invested a lot of time in there.

00:04:10.022 --> 00:04:25.508
But because Jung was such a close friend of Freud, I guess he also has a lot of unique insights on not only Freud's theories, but also his personality.

00:04:26.354 --> 00:04:37.914
How he was privately, and I will just talk a little bit about Jung's life and how he came into contact with Freud and how their friendship started.

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:56.902
So when Jung had to decide what to study, he was unsure what to do, and he, uh, was thinking about doing archaeology because he was very interested in the past, passion that stick with him. His whole life, or whether he should go into medicine.

00:04:57.144 --> 00:05:19.762
And this was something that troubled him a lot because he wa— he knew he wanted to study and he wanted to study in Basel, but it was a dream that finally convinced him what to do, that he should pursue the path of medicine. And there he got into contact with psychology as a field.

00:05:19.931 --> 00:05:44.852
And back then, this is around 1900, psychology was a completely new field. It was the newest science. It was the bleeding edge, like the Silicon Valley IT startup crypto space of, okay, we are now exploring a new realm of the world and Nothing is really clear.

00:05:44.900 --> 00:06:05.264
It's really like discovering a new continent, and he uses that metaphor very often. So there's a clear distinction between the world before psychology and after psychology, the discovery of the psyche. And one very clear distinction is that how patients were treated.

00:06:05.811 --> 00:06:25.865
So when somebody would have psychological problems, The going theory was that they had brain damage, and when somebody has brain damage, there's basically nothing you can do. And so they would just put the people somewhere just to store them like furniture and wait until they would die someday.

00:06:26.283 --> 00:06:46.536
But there was not really an attempt to, to treat them or really a successful way to treat them. And Jung, grew up in that atmosphere where this was a change of, okay, there might be something more to the patients than just the, the problems and the symptoms that they have.

00:06:47.468 --> 00:07:05.668
And he would be very involved also in analyzing the brains of people who had died that had psychological problems. And he could not find any statistical relevant damage of the brains. That you could say, okay, this part is rotten. This is why the person cannot live normally.

00:07:06.008 --> 00:07:16.785
So he got very suspicious about the orthodoxy back then, and he would start doing his own experiments.

00:07:16.849 --> 00:07:39.743
So he would study medicine, he would study psychology, and he would start working in Burghölzli, which was a psychiatric institution in Switzerland, and he would start to design his own experiments to get his own data about delving into the psyche of people.

00:07:40.533 --> 00:07:57.745
And the method that he's invented is called the association test. And the association test works in the way that you give people a word, for example, family, a house, child, money, and you would try to get the reaction.

00:07:58.724 --> 00:08:22.936
So the idea is the patient is just giving word, word, word, word, word as a stimulus, and they would give back the reaction. And of course, the word would be noted that the patient said, but also the time that it took the patient to say the word. And he worked on that for a very long time. And, uh, got some really interesting results.

00:08:23.987 --> 00:08:45.969
And this was this investigation into the psyche because there was a certain phenomena that people normally, they would get into a kind of groove and they would say a reaction within a certain timeframe, very short seconds. But sometimes when he would say a word, there would be a long pause.

00:08:46.632 --> 00:09:09.419
And sometimes the patients would even forget the word and they would give some reaction, but very often delayed. And the smart thing that Jung did was to make the same test again, same words, same order, but very often the patients, the word that they said that was so delayed, that came where the answer came very late, they used a different word.

00:09:10.702 --> 00:09:16.298
So everything was the same, but then they used something else. Or he would point out, oh, you said that word, and they could not remember.

00:09:17.164 --> 00:09:34.211
And this made him suspicious that there would be something in the psyche acting outside of the willful control of the person, that the person was not aware what the person would say, that there would trigger something happening in the background that would be distracting and autonomous.

00:09:35.221 --> 00:09:49.233
And back then, there was not really a word for that, and the word came through Freud, and this was the unconscious, that there's background processes happening that we are not aware of, but they're influencing our lives.

00:09:51.739 --> 00:10:15.892
And Jung was doing these, uh, experiments already for a pretty long time and was following the literature of all the people who would publish about the topics. And he said Freud was the first person who could come up with something as a concept, a concept of the mind that would explain the empirical findings of Carl Gustav Jung.

00:10:16.309 --> 00:10:32.391
And of course, Jung was immediately a huge fan of Freud and he would write all these letters to him and he would argue with people about psychoanalysis and he will become very well-versed in psychoanalysis. And finally, he had the chance.

00:10:32.632 --> 00:10:53.768
To meet Freud and for the first time that they met, uh, met, they spoke for 13 hours straight. They just talked and talked and talked and talked for 13 hours. And this was the beginning of the friendship. Jung back then was in his mid-30s, early 30s rather.

00:10:54.479 --> 00:11:12.113
And Freud was 20 years older. He was in his 50s. So there was this age difference and also this difference a little bit of who is, let's say, well known or who's hyped right now.

00:11:12.517 --> 00:11:34.391
And Freud was very popular back then because of his book of The Interpretation of Dreams and his ideas of psychoanalysis and the unconscious, especially in, um, Europe. Interestingly, the ideas and methods of Jung became very popular in the US.

00:11:34.696 --> 00:11:57.037
So they traveled also around, also to the US, and there Jung was more well-known and popular than Freud back then. But this was, would shift over time. So they became incredibly close. They wrote a lot. In regards to letters to each other. They would analyze each other's dreams.

00:11:57.406 --> 00:12:13.174
So it was really Freud's hope and idea that Jung, because he was 20 years younger, could over the, take over the scepter and bring psychoanalysis into the world. Because Freud had a little bit of chip on his shoulder.

00:12:14.360 --> 00:12:37.876
He had to convince the world of something which is very hard to understand and to believe, to say, okay, you're not the lord in your own house. And to say, okay, there is all these forces inside you and you need to deal with them. And there's also outside help necessary to do this.

00:12:40.167 --> 00:13:00.700
And as they work together, Jung himself said he followed Freud also like a father figure, not only because of the age, but also Jung was a little bit disillusioned with his own father who died, uh, pretty young, uh, in Jung's life.

00:13:02.485 --> 00:13:14.287
And there was this teacher-student dynamic happening there, even though that Jung, as I said, he was established. He published his own papers, did his own research.

00:13:15.050 --> 00:13:37.915
He was already a doctor or professor teaching, but this is the part that many people know when you talk about Jung and Freud, when they say, oh yeah, this is a, it's a pupil of Freud, but that's not completely correct. This is how they showed up, but Jung was established as a psychologist and as a therapist all on his own.

00:13:39.540 --> 00:14:03.051
But over the years, Jung realized that there are some differences in worldview and understanding, that he thought of Freud's idea as a little bit restricting and simple in certain regards. And Jung was always very interested in the occult, the spiritual, religious. And Freud would have nothing of that.

00:14:03.324 --> 00:14:23.443
He would be not interested in that at all because he saw himself really as a fighter who could not deviate from his idea about psychoanalysis and libido as source of psychic functioning.

00:14:24.181 --> 00:14:46.974
He did not want to have any Strange ideas in there that could distract from the ideas of psychoanalysis, but this was something that was gnawing on Carl Gustav Jung and it got only worse with time. And as I said before, they were analyzing each other's dreams, but with time they would start hiding their dreams from each other.

00:14:49.010 --> 00:15:08.590
So, uh, one started with Freud, who said, okay, I have a, I have a dream. I had a dream, but I did not want to talk with you about it because I felt it would undermine my authority. And this is when Jung got suspicious.

00:15:08.963 --> 00:15:29.111
And Jung also had a specific dream about a house, which not only had a cellar, but multiple cellars where you could traveled back in time where it was the 1800s, then the 1000s, then ancient Rome, and in final, like a Neanderthal cave.

00:15:30.347 --> 00:15:53.729
And then he already had the feeling, ah, I cannot tell this dream to Freud because he would interpret these things into there. So they were already living apart a bit. And The relationship ended after roughly 6 and 7 years in a very dramatic way, how doctors and professors end friendships by writing a book.

00:15:54.117 --> 00:16:15.807
And Jung wrote a book called Symbols of Transformation. It's the 5th book of the Collected Works. The original title was a little bit different, but the modern name is Symbols of Transformation, and it had all the stuff in it. That Freud did not want to talk about. It was about religion, mythology, spirituality, and all those things.

00:16:16.517 --> 00:16:40.489
And he sent it to Freud and he only got a letter back that said something like, okay, seems like you want to do your own thing now. It's a code for me. But what it meant was the end of their friendship, but also of Jung's career, and he knew this. So his academic career was done.

00:16:42.140 --> 00:16:55.527
He was tightly associated with Freud. Freud was at that time very powerful, and he cuts Jung just completely off. So Jung could not teach anymore, and he withdrew.

00:16:56.073 --> 00:17:08.607
He still had his private practice, but he knew that his career that he worked for basically his whole life was with that over, but he had the feeling that there was the right thing to do.

00:17:09.329 --> 00:17:30.999
And if you think about it, the things that everybody knows about Jung, the collective unconscious, The Archetypes and the Collective Unbewusstes, but also Aions or the views on alchemy, all this came afterwards. Before it was Jung, the psychoanalyst, but afterwards it became Jung, the phenomenon that is the reason why we still talk about him now.

00:17:31.707 --> 00:17:53.805
And interestingly, in the Collected Works, the first four books are about this block in Jung's life, about his work in psychoanalysis. This is a block where I think it's the least read part, but it is highly, highly interesting to understand. C.G. Jung and where he's coming from.

00:17:55.190 --> 00:18:16.866
And this is also why I wanted to talk about this, to have the background to understand that. So to give an introduction into Freudian psychoanalysis, as I said, it's a easier way to do it. You have to think of yourself as the ego.

00:18:18.117 --> 00:18:42.091
The person which is experiencing things with the id under it, where you have all the primal forces and instincts that are trying to act on the ego. And you have the superego, which is the rules of society and your, the laws, how you were educated as a child.

00:18:43.184 --> 00:19:06.949
They act like a selector and they say, oh, this is a good instinct to have. This is not a good instinct to have. Oh, this is not appropriate now. And everything that gets discarded gets discarded into the unconscious. So the Freudian idea is that the unconscious is like a wastebasket where you have a lot of things that can accumulate there.

00:19:07.558 --> 00:19:20.330
And if you discard the wrong things, Uh, yeah, discard the right things. This can create trouble in the psyche of the person and prevent the ego from working normally.

00:19:23.370 --> 00:19:35.004
And these things in the unconscious are complexes that can cause neuroses or hysteria, which brings the person to act.

00:19:35.576 --> 00:20:01.328
In strange ways, but through talking with the patient through these problems, to take the things from the wastebasket and bring them to light, there's a way to heal the person of the complexes, of the hysteria, and make them functioning normally again, that they're part of society.

00:20:02.054 --> 00:20:20.258
Part of life. And this was a revolutionary thing. As I mentioned before, the idea was, oh, you're damaged goods, there's nothing you can do. And the idea from Freud is, and this is why it's called the talking cure, you can talk with people through their problems and you can heal them.

00:20:20.868 --> 00:20:42.422
And not only in their psyche, but also bodily, that there are certain symptoms that disappear when you help people working through their problems and talking about the things that are in the unconscious. And there he would often use hypnosis.

00:20:43.488 --> 00:21:06.080
So this was a tool which was not really used back then before, but he would hypnotize the patients or try to hypnotize them to open up the unconscious and to bring out these very dark contents, because these are contents that were not approved of from the parents of the patient, of, of society.

00:21:07.995 --> 00:21:32.609
And the complexes that Freud saw in this wastebasket, they were very often associated with the parents, with the mother, with the father. And he said the main drive of the id is libido, is sexuality, the sexual drive. And this sexual drive needs to be steered in the correct direction.

00:21:32.962 --> 00:21:51.640
And he said the natural way how this libido normally flows is towards the mother, sexuality towards the mother or towards the father. But this is frowned upon by society. So it goes into the wastebasket, but it should not be. In the wastebasket, it should be used differently.

00:21:52.106 --> 00:22:15.075
So the idea of Freudian psychology is to say, okay, you have to find your mother in a partner, or you have to find your father in a job to steer this energy in a productive outward direction, not inward towards the family, but outward to the world. So this is called the, the incest complex.

00:22:16.402 --> 00:22:31.493
That is blocking the, the patient and living their own life. Uh, and he compares this to an infantilization, that people are still like a child. So even though they should grow up.

00:22:35.020 --> 00:22:46.416
For this, he also used Dream Interpretation, and this is one of the other things that Freud brought back into the modern world to use dreams as a diagnostic tool.

00:22:46.865 --> 00:23:06.831
So this was lost for hundreds, thousands of years because dreams are a little bit of individual revelation, which is a big no-no in Christianity, but had a higher value in ancient societies like Roman societies, Greek societies, and earlier Egyptian societies.

00:23:08.370 --> 00:23:13.689
So he rediscovered that and he established that as a valid diagnostic tool.

00:23:13.738 --> 00:23:35.541
And this is— should you read The Interpretation of Dreams, you would find it there that he's very meticulous and takes it incredibly serious and has a lot of concepts there that you can even try yourself and you can find out, oh, okay, yeah, the, what Mr. Freud wrote over 100 years ago, you can find in your own dreams. So this is interesting. Really interesting.

00:23:39.429 --> 00:23:49.557
This is just to give you a rough overview about psychoanalysis and about all these points that I just mentioned.

00:23:50.250 --> 00:24:08.404
Jung started to develop different ideas, and this had to start with the idea of sexuality because Freud talked about sexuality in a way broader sense than the word would suggest. So we have a pretty clear idea what sexuality means.

00:24:10.846 --> 00:24:25.336
But for Freud, he used it so broadly that Jung even doubted whether it would be still the correct word, or rather that it would be the survival instinct or the instinct to, um, continue living.

00:24:26.133 --> 00:24:43.812
Because in, in Freudian psychology, the idea is that the first burst of sexuality is when you are a small child and you, for example, you will want to eat the whole time. And then you kind of forget your sexuality until you hit puberty, where it's the second surge of sexuality.

00:24:44.663 --> 00:25:09.261
And Jung said, okay, this is maybe a little bit too restrictive to call that sexuality. So Jung took the libido term and used it rather as a psychic energy to have, okay, I have, it's neutral in that regard. I can use it in, in any way. It can manifest in any way. But he did not want to talk about hunger only in a sexual context.

00:25:09.631 --> 00:25:26.263
Also, this is a little bit confusing because everybody already has a pretty clear idea of what sexuality means. What Freud would also do, he would try to reduce ideas a lot.

00:25:26.648 --> 00:25:49.939
So the reason why he would not like to talk about religion, for example, was because he said, yeah, well, in its basis, it's just a father complex. You want to be liked by your father and you're in awe of, with your father. And this is why you like religion because religion, it has rules. And we need rules because the rules is the reasons why we don't sleep with our parents.

00:25:52.366 --> 00:26:14.274
And well, for Freud, it's really this more and more simplification of the world where he said, oh yeah, Hadrian built the wall in Great Britain because he wanted to impress his wife to have sex. Meh. Yeah. People started religion because they were in awe with their father. Of course you can try to reduce and reduce it like that.

00:26:15.027 --> 00:26:37.365
Jung said it's not the right approach to take the complexity of the world and just bring it down to singular factor. And Freud said, okay, well, all the problems stem from the incest complex. And so, yeah, okay, possibly, but does it really account for the whole experience of human beings and does it really explain anything? Because it's very easy to reduce something.

00:26:37.783 --> 00:26:50.907
But you cannot say, okay, there exists something like sexuality and this is why Hadrian built the wall. There has to be steps in between, and these were missing for Jung.

00:26:52.930 --> 00:27:03.588
And another fault that Freud had just as his way of working was that Freud would never revise or correct.

00:27:03.653 --> 00:27:27.930
This goes back to this idea of establishing himself as a new force that cannot be distracted by something else, or that cannot come up in a confused manner. It has to be always clear. It has also always to be on point. It can only be this one thing. It has to be psychoanalysis.

00:27:28.011 --> 00:27:42.752
It has to be sexuality. And what many people know about psychoanalysis is, is, oh, there was a trauma in childhood and this is why the grownup person now has a problem. And this was an early idea of psychoanalysis.

00:27:43.250 --> 00:27:59.826
And Freud wrote about this as a source of hysteria, because when he talked to the patients, then the patients would start talking about childhood experiences that would come up through psychoanalysis. And he said, oh, okay, this explains why this grown person now has a problem.

00:28:02.030 --> 00:28:13.688
But as Freud would reveal over time in letters to Jung, these cases that he used as a basis for that later turned out never actually happened.

00:28:15.730 --> 00:28:37.973
There's a phenomena that the patients immediately wanted to go to childhood themselves and revealed this information, revealed fantasies that did not actually happened, which were the ground why Freud started with his ideas. But at the same time, Freud did not want to revise himself in that regard that he would fundamentally change concepts.

00:28:38.648 --> 00:28:58.483
So he would still write that and publish that, or let's say the original works, keep them unchanged, which caused, of course, a little bit of confusion. And Jung had to correct a lot of the critics of psychoanalysis that he said, okay, this is not valid anymore. This is not even how Freud thinks anymore.

00:29:00.647 --> 00:29:25.584
And the logic that Jung built on that to say, okay, why do patients have the need to talk about their childhood? Why suddenly these patient fantasies in the past come up? For him was rather the idea that the patient is failing at a task that is existing right now.

00:29:26.436 --> 00:29:45.448
And his metaphor was when somebody gets tuberculosis, it's not because they had contact with the tuberculosis bacteria in their childhood. They had it just now. So his idea was that there was a task that the person needed to do. But could not do for whatever reason.

00:29:47.600 --> 00:30:11.435
So the libido, the neutral in the Jungian sense, force, psychic force, instead of pushing forward, would hit a barrier and flow backward. So it would start activating all these old contents of the past, which would kind of distract the person from the actual problem that is lying right before them. So it's like a distraction mechanism.

00:30:13.070 --> 00:30:34.264
For example, that people were upset that they did not marry the right person, right? Or they were not even married. They could not find a partner. They could not find a job. Important people around them died. And that this was the task at hand that need to be solved and not to be distracted by the past.

00:30:35.190 --> 00:30:57.242
To talk about that and talk through it, but there needs to be something differently done to change it now. And what Jung did not like about the Freudian psychoanalysis had to do with unearthing these things.

00:30:57.584 --> 00:31:22.481
So the Freudians that later worked were in their mind very more strict than Freud himself because they oriented more on the books, it seems like. So a lot of things in psychoanalysis is about resistance from the patient, that the patient does not want to talk about the thing because it's the mother complex or the incest complex or the father complex.

00:31:23.381 --> 00:31:36.882
And Jung said, okay, this is not the right approach because he He saw it more that the therapist would try to dig for complexes because it was certain there had to be the incest complex because it's the center of the psyche, the reason why people do things.

00:31:37.908 --> 00:31:57.868
So it would rather be pushing and steering and directing the patient in a certain direction to hit something, to find the complex, because for Freud was really, okay, if I take the complex out of the wastebasket and bring it to light. And give it a name, very important thing, it will lose its power.

00:31:58.751 --> 00:32:16.976
Freud even went so far that he said one day there will be no more neuroses because we found all the names of the complexes that are in the psyche, which is a kind of romantic idea to say, okay, there's an end because we just named everything. But this was how Freud operated according to Jung.

00:32:18.759 --> 00:32:37.973
So how the therapist would work with a patient, this is also something that Jung did not like. As in a Freudian sense, the therapist is in the background, not even seen. He's behind the patient just asking questions.

00:32:38.585 --> 00:33:01.260
So, and the patient would just say what they think, the associations, and follow those associations. So this, the idea was, okay. At a certain point, we will hit a complex, then we will be in the unconscious, and then we can bring it to light. But what Jung did not like about it was the complete removal of the therapist as a person.

00:33:01.758 --> 00:33:22.087
So the Jungian way of doing therapy would be a rather more personal way. Also not to give the patient a free flow of associations that just build on top of each other. But rather to find association about a specific topic and build on those to explore specific topic more and not go from piece to piece to piece to piece to piece.

00:33:24.760 --> 00:33:43.857
And the biggest, the absolute biggest problem that Jung had with Freud, and which was likely one of the main reasons why they separated ways, was Freud's ideas about dreams. Jung took dreams incredibly seriously.

00:33:44.829 --> 00:34:02.777
Freud also did, but the theories of Freud, they're a little bit hard to get. So I read The Interpretation of Dreams. I'm still not completely sure that I understood it correctly. The idea is that there's something in the back of the mind of every person, and that's called the censor.

00:34:04.049 --> 00:34:28.098
So when people dream, the goal is that the person does not wake up. It's like a fulfillment of a wish. So what the censor will do, it will try to hide the contents of the dream behind a facade to not upset the dreamer, to keep it dreaming. So it will tell the dreamer a lie so that the dreamer will keep on dreaming.

00:34:28.179 --> 00:34:45.831
And The idea is interesting in that sense that psychoanalysis has this as a staple, as a basic premise, that the first thing you encounter does not have to be the real thing in the background.

00:34:46.961 --> 00:34:57.900
As I said before, people that had psychological problems, symptoms, even bodily, it was never about those symptoms, but rather, okay, there has to be some reason behind it.

00:34:58.913 --> 00:35:15.208
That is causing this, and Freud also applied this to the dreams to say, okay, you dream of a grown man in a, in an overcoat, and this is something sexual because the overcoat could be interpreted as a penis.

00:35:18.455 --> 00:35:38.340
And this of course then would go back to the incest complex and, and so on. This would be the Freudian way how to get to there. And Jung said, okay, this, this can't be completely correct because what Freud is doing again, he reduces, reduces, reduces to say, okay, you have an overcoat versus a penis versus incest complex.

00:35:38.857 --> 00:35:56.170
But Jung said, okay, why, when you have all these possibilities in the world, what it could be, why is this person dreaming of this? When somebody's dreaming of a fairy, we don't have to say, okay, this is representing your mother. We have to ask the question, why is it a fairy?

00:35:56.833 --> 00:36:14.752
It could be anything in the world because when you can reduce anything to a mother complex, there are 10,000 ways to do it. But why does the mind come up with a fairy? And he said, it's very dishonest to say, okay, everything is a facade because then you cannot really be sure what you're actually treating.

00:36:18.166 --> 00:36:36.432
So in the end, it becomes a little bit vague what the therapist then actually does because he has all this evidence, but he ignores the evidence and say, oh, well, actually there's something behind it, but we cannot see it. So it's like treating a disease that you can't really measure or, or find directly.

00:36:38.920 --> 00:37:00.684
And this is, I made a whole episode about the complexes, about Jungian Dream Interpretation as a diagnostic tool, which is very interesting, very hard to get into because Jung tries to combine all this complexity into it. But Freud is, okay, reduce it down, find the complex, name the complex, bring it to light, and then it's done.

00:37:03.308 --> 00:37:26.764
When I talked before about the concept that things come from the id to the ego and then they get discarded. Into the unconscious. This means that everything that's in the unconscious first had to be conscious because it has to be repressed to be in the unconscious. So it has to be filtered out by the rules, norms of society, how the person grew up.

00:37:28.009 --> 00:37:46.977
And Jung said that's not true. People can have things in their unconscious that they're not even aware of. So His idea is not that the unconscious comes from consciousness, but rather the other way around, that consciousness is a product of the unconscious.

00:37:51.939 --> 00:38:11.636
And his overall view was that Freud was more like a destructor, and this was important in the world back then. You have to see when psychology was new. Before the World War II was the Victorian age. It was very materialistic.

00:38:11.684 --> 00:38:27.211
That's why people thought, okay, the brain is damaged because it was more of a way of seeing the world through a materialistic lens. And also very repressed sense. The Victorian age where people would not even talk about sex with their own partners.

00:38:28.090 --> 00:38:48.787
So the value of Freud, Jung saw as a great liberator, as a historic necessity to give people suddenly the room to talk about sexuality, to create a space where this is okay. The things that have been actually be ignored or repressed before, to give them a stage.

00:38:49.766 --> 00:39:09.247
And he also said the value of hypnosis was that, to give people a stage to talk about it. It was not so important whether hypnosis works or not. The idea, the premise to say, okay, yeah, when you say something in hypnosis, that's not really you, it's your unconscious, already gave people a way to get away from this repression.

00:39:09.761 --> 00:39:25.422
So even when it sounded, what I talked about, the ideas of Freud, that it's very negative in Jung's view. This is just arguing about some facets and some single ideas and viewpoints.

00:39:26.228 --> 00:39:50.743
The basic, uh, basis of Jungian psychoanalysis or analytical psychology, how Jung called it, is deeply, deeply rooted in psychoanalysis. So there are many things they agree on. There's a lot of common ground. There are some things that Jung does not criticize or ignore.

00:39:51.049 --> 00:40:10.690
He never talks about there is not something like the unconscious, and he doesn't say there is not something like the ego. He says that dreams are a valid tool of interpretation, and there are psychic forces that act on us, and that sexuality, of course, is a part of the psyche, but there are more forces working on that.

00:40:12.434 --> 00:40:33.624
He even credits Freud, by discovering the very first archetype, the Oedipal complex, the, the boy who wants to sleep with his mother, the incest complex. He says this is something that's not even personal. It's even lower than that. It's in the collective unconscious. It's an archetype.

00:40:33.705 --> 00:40:57.192
It's something that people live through actually, but it's not that it's born on the individual experience of the person, but rather it's the collective part under there. The metaphor would be we think we live in a house and Freud discovered there's a cellar under it that we are not aware of and where things happen, strange things.

00:40:58.186 --> 00:41:20.106
And Jung found out, okay, under there, there are tunnels that connect all the cellars to each other that this, there's a collective part. So Freud very much stays with the personal. He says, oh, the rules that you get, the superego, this is your personal experience you got from your parents and the country you live in, the government.

00:41:23.634 --> 00:41:37.561
And yeah, you have these forces, but they're very simple, the id and when you have something in your unconscious, it's only personal stuff. And Jung says, oh no, no, this is, this is way more complex.

00:41:37.867 --> 00:41:49.048
You have to think about primal things, things that move centuries and millennia and cultures and countries and events all around the world, artwork and so on.

00:41:51.270 --> 00:42:09.419
It is also, you can find this in, in the naming of Jung's brand of psychoanalysis is analytical psychology. It's not a completely different thing, but rather it's a switching on the terms. It's a variation about the basic structures.

00:42:09.531 --> 00:42:32.448
This is why I think it's also very important to read the first four books of the Collected Works to see, okay, where is Jung coming from? And this is why he insisted on, okay, if you want to understand me, it's the best idea to see how everything grew. Because even when he talks about archetypes, when he talks about Aions or alchemy, it's all rooted on a psychoanalytic basis.

00:42:33.192 --> 00:42:51.884
And I was similar in that regard. When I read the Collected Works, I started with the fun stuff. I started with Symbols of Transformation and The Archetypes and the Collective Unconscious and all those things. And then I said, okay, I want to read everything.

00:42:51.933 --> 00:43:05.465
So I read the first 4 books and it blew my mind because to see these ideas always stick with Jung and they were always around. The idea that there are complexes that work within us.

00:43:05.739 --> 00:43:21.464
So the idea of analytical psychology was first to call it complex psychology and that a neurosis has to be lived out, that it has to be brought to life, this complex. Jung also fully agreed. He practiced his whole life like that.

00:43:23.790 --> 00:43:45.332
And he also felt that psychoanalysis was very misunderstood by the critics and everyone else, even though that they were separated as persons. They, they afterwards, they had close to no contact. They, they did some referrals for patients, but otherwise they really had no contact anymore for the rest of their lives.

00:43:46.483 --> 00:44:07.208
He still stayed with Psychoanalysis and bringing this forward, but to build on it continuously. So this is something that has to be kept in mind.

00:44:08.620 --> 00:44:32.161
To talk a little bit about Freud as a person, how Jung saw him. He saw Freud as a neurotic person, interestingly, that was struggling internally a lot. And he saw Freud also as a very introverted person, but an introverted feeling person.

00:44:32.210 --> 00:44:52.987
So those who are familiar with Jung's typology, The feeling function is about valuing things, to give a value judgment. Okay, do I like it? Don't I like it? And he said there Freud was very proficient and very differentiated, sophisticated.

00:44:54.407 --> 00:45:17.865
But where he saw Freud inferior was the thinking. Interesting enough, where you would say that Freud would not have necessarily the right word or the right thinking to express actually what he feels, but he focused on that because he was neurotic scrambling his psyche a little bit.

00:45:21.020 --> 00:45:38.747
But he credits Freud with the achievements that he's done, and he really disliked the people who followed Freud because those people were systematic and incredibly strict, and they argued not so much how Freud would do so.

00:45:39.390 --> 00:45:58.392
As I mentioned before, in private, Freud was more open to, to other things and also to alternative variations of his own ideas. But in public, he was incredibly strict about what he would think and how his knowledge should be transported. And Jung goes on and on and on.

00:45:58.570 --> 00:46:11.219
I read his letters about the Freudians who make a little bit of a character of themselves, especially as he knew Freud best, but they started trying to tell him what Freud actually meant.

00:46:13.560 --> 00:46:32.804
So this relationship that they had was an unusual relationship because They were very different people, very different people. For one thing, Freud was Jewish and Jung was Protestant, not really a believer, but he grew up like that.

00:46:34.076 --> 00:46:58.057
Jung was younger, Freud was older, and this worldview of theirs was too different to, to bring it together. So it's amazing that they worked together like this for a very long time. But as I said, it was because Jung really took himself back up to the point where he could not stand it anymore and he had to break free and find his own way.

00:46:58.860 --> 00:47:20.846
And if you're interested finding more about their dynamic, there's a movie about it called A Dangerous Method with Viggo Mortensen, Michael Fassbender, and Keira Knightley, which is really interesting movie to see because it's really about this time and it really ends with the end. Of their friendship, but it is about Jung trying to establish himself.

00:47:22.000 --> 00:47:46.361
Jung was also very rich because he married the second richest woman in Switzerland back then. Freud was always very poor. And to have those work together must have been really interesting to see. But Jung would always agree with Freud.

